# SP+ — the advisor, and therefore Fin, may administer this machine without a
# password prompt.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Fin is the advisor's assistant and runs AS the advisor. Asked
# to add a printer, install something, or read a system log, Fin's only route is
# sudo -- and it cannot answer a password prompt. Worse, the advisor frequently
# cannot either: SP+ ships no account at all (DN-13), the account is created by
# the first-boot wizard, and a password chosen once during setup is not
# something a non-technical user reliably remembers months later. Requiring it
# turns every genuine repair into a dead end at the exact moment the advisor
# needs help.
#
# WHAT THIS COSTS, stated plainly. Anyone at an unlocked session is effectively
# root. SP+ accepts that deliberately: the containment here is architectural
# rather than a permission wall. /usr is read-only, every change lands in a new
# deployment, a bad state is one `bootc rollback` away, and the disk is LUKS
# encrypted so an unattended machine is protected by being locked, not by sudo.
# A password prompt the user cannot answer buys no real security -- it only
# stops the assistant from helping.
#
# This grants nothing that the advisor, as the owner of the machine, does not
# already have the right to do. It removes a prompt, not a boundary.
%wheel ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL
